course: dictionary
lesson: 10681
title: Unpleasant and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unpleasant means not nice or not enjoyable. Read the words around it to be sure. «The smell from the bin was unpleasant.» A bin is not a nice place to be, so the smell is not nice either. Swap in a nice word, like pleasant, and the sentence stops making sense.
explain: The same word can describe a person. «The man was unpleasant to the waiter.» Here unpleasant means unkind or rude. Ask yourself what would fit in the sentence: a kind word would not fit, so the clue points to rude.
explain: Writers sometimes use a soft word for a hard thing. Unpleasantness can mean a quarrel, a fight or a war, but it sounds gentler than those words. When you meet it, read the rest of the text to find out what it really means.
explain: Words can change shape too. Unpleasantly is an adverb, describing how something happens, and unpleasantness is a noun, a thing you can name. Spotting the shape helps you guess the meaning.
fact: Unpleasant means not nice or not enjoyable.
fact: «An unpleasant person» is someone who is unkind or rude.
fact: «Unpleasantness» can be a polite word for a fight, a quarrel or a war.
fact: «Bite the bullet» means face something unpleasant bravely; the story about soldiers biting bullets is often told but not proven.
fact: Unpleasantly is an adverb, and unpleasantness is a noun made from unpleasant.
exercise: «The smell from the drains was unpleasant.» What does unpleasant mean here? | Soft and warm | Bright and shiny | *Not nice to smell | Very loud
exercise: Which word is a noun made from unpleasant? | unpleasant | pleasant | *unpleasantness | unpleasantly
exercise: «Bite the bullet» means what? | Eat something hard | Bite into a hard sweet | *Face something unpleasant bravely | Run away quickly
test: «The man was unpleasant to the waiter.» What does unpleasant mean here? | Happy and smiling | *Unkind or rude | Quiet and shy | Pleasant and friendly
test: An author writes, «the unpleasantness between the two countries». What might unpleasantness mean? | A lovely holiday | A big party | A kind gift | *A quarrel or a war
exam: Why might a writer use «unpleasantness» instead of the harsh word «war»? | Because it means a sunny day | Because it is the longest word | Because it means a happy party | *Because it is a softer, polite word for a harsh thing
